===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|weyd}}
From {{bor|en|fro|prudence}}, from {{der|en|la|prūdentia}}, alternative form of {{m|la|prōvidentia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹuːdəns/}}
* {{hyph|en|pru|dence}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Mélange a trois-prudence.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The quality or state of being [[prudent]]; wisdom in the way of caution and provision; [[discretion]]; [[carefulness]]; hence, also, [[economy]]; [[frugality]].
#* '''1876''', [[w:Samuel Austin Allibone|Samuel Austin Allibone]], ''Prose Quotations from Socrates to Macaulay'', J.B. Lippincott, page 597,
#*: '''Prudence''' is principally in reference to actions to be done, and due means, order, seasons, and method of doing or not doing. - [[w:Sir Matthew Hale|Sir Matthew Hale]].
#*: '''''Prudence''' supposes the value of the end to be assumed, and refers only to the adaptation of the means. It is the relation of right means for given ends.'' - [[w:William Whewell|William Whewell]].

